Pandas Essentials:

1. Series:
    one-dimensional labeled array (labeled with index; numeriacal automatically, labelled to be done manually)
    ages = pd.Series([25, 30, 35, 40]) # index 0 to 3
    ages_labelled = pd.Series([25, 30, 35, 40], index=['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'David'])

2. DadaFrame
    two-dimensional labeled data structure (rows x columns)
    Eg: a table of people with age and city

3. Indexing with .loc (df['Name'] indicates column selection, df.loc and df.iloc indicates rows selection)
    end index is inclusive
    uses row/column label 
    useful when you have named indices or column names
    df.loc[[0, 2]] # gives first and third rows 

4. Indexing with .iloc
    end index is exclusive
    
5. Boolean indexing:
    Filters rows based on condition
    Eg print age > 27
    print(df[df['Age'] > 27])

    eg City is 'Butwal'
    print(df[df['City'] == 'Butwal'])

6. Chained Assignments and SettingWithCopyWarning
    Instead of writing df[df['City'] == 'Butwal']['Age'] = 99 
        # Pandas confused whether you are slicing or writing
    Write df.loc[df['City'] == 'Butwal', 'Age'] = 99
        # Select rows where City = "Butwal" and set age = 99 in those rows

7. MultiIndex objects:
    Instead of just one index (like row number), you can have multiple levels
    eg City -> Name -> Age 
    Makes grouped data analysis easier

    # Set multiIndex: City + Name
    df_multi = df.set_index(["City", "Name"])

    # Access data with multiIndex 
    # Select all rows where City = "Palpa"
    print(df_multi.loc["Palpa"])
    # Select a specific person (city + Name)
    print(df_multi.loc[("Palpa", "Hari")])